# Chapter 2, part 3: New, Delete, Import, Export, messages, examples.
.h2 New [file-new]
.ix New
.ix New File panel
.ix F12, File, New
.ix Creating a document, New command
.ix Document type, structured or unstructured
.ix Template, New command
New starts a new document without leaving the Editor.  The document that was
open is closed (after a question, if it had unsaved changes) and the Editor
shows the new one.  The new document is a copy of a template, or of the
built-in settings when there is no template, and it has the name
given.  When there are templates, New begins with a list of them
({n:file-new-list}).  Nothing is written to the disk: the file comes to
exist when the document is first saved.
.table 20 40
Way to reach it | Notes
{k:F12}, File, New | Press {k:F12}, then {k:F} and {k:N}.
.end
There is no key of its own for New.  The menu hint reads "Create a new
document".
.pic pictures/file-new.png 5 The New File panel.
.h3 Ways to begin a document [file-new-ways]
.ix Creating a file
.ix Starting a document
.ix File does not exist until it is saved
There are two ways to begin a document.  In all of them nothing is
written on the disk until the document is saved: a new document has a name,
and that is all.  If the Editor is left without saving, the file does not
come to exist.
.table 22 38
Way | What happens
The Document Manager, Edit / Preview /
 Print Document, | A prompt offers the choice
 with a name that does not exist | between a structured and an unstructured document;
 | the Description and the Author
 | are requested, and the Editor is
 | started on the name.
File, New (inside the Editor) | The panel New File, described below.
.end
The Document Manager and the New command add {c:.doc} to a name that has no
dot, so that the name {c:plan} makes {c:plan.doc}.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: the Editor started by hand with the name of a file that does
not exist opens at once with a new structured document called by that name,
exactly as given (the name {c:plan} makes a document called {c:plan}, with no
extension); with the option {c:-u} (or {c:--unstructured}) the document is
unstructured.
.endif
.h3 The new document [file-new-defaults]
.ix New document, contents
.ix Default settings of a new document
.ix Structured and unstructured, choice at creation
A document that is made with no template has one section and one empty block.
A structured one has, in addition, the root section above it.  Its settings
are these.
.table 31 29
Setting | Value
Revision | 1.000
Paper | Letter, 8.5 by 11 inches
Page number tag | {c:\1\} (the tags of the title page and
 | the table of contents are {c:\i\})
Title page, vertical centering | 33 percent
Headline attributes | Bold
Text justification | Even
Tab stops of the text | 5
Spacing, headline and block | 1.50 each
Spacing, subsection and peer | 2.00 each
Indent per level | 3
.end
The other settings of the Global menu have the values that their panels show.
To begin with other settings, use a template ({n:file-templates}).
.caution A new document counts as changed from the first moment, because the
  New command has given it its name, description and revision.  The
  status line shows an asterisk before the name, and Quit puts the question whether to save
  although nothing has been typed.
.h3 The list of templates [file-new-list]
.ix Templates, list
.ix Description of a template
.ix New, list of templates
When there is at least one template in the directories of {x:file-templates-path},
New first shows a list.  The first two entries are the built-in settings,
Structured and Unstructured; the others are the templates, in the order of
their names.  The lines below the list show the description of the entry
that is chosen, and a template that has none says so.  {k:Up} and {k:Down}
choose, {k:Enter} or {k:Ins} takes the entry, and {k:Esc} takes the built-in
settings.  In the Editor the New File panel follows, with the entry in its
field Template.  The Document Manager shows the same list when it is given
the name of a document that does not exist; there {k:Ins} makes the document
at once from the entry, with no panel and with the description and the author
left blank, and {k:Enter} shows the New File panel first.  The description of
a template is the Description of the document that is the template, the one
that the Save File and New File panels have; it has at most 250 characters and
no control character, and a document that has more or has one is not opened.
In the New File panel the field Template has the same list: {k:F2} (or
{k:Keypad +}) opens it.  {k:Ins} on an entry puts it in the field and finishes
the panel, as {k:Ins} does in any panel, so that the new document is made at
once; {k:Enter} on an entry only puts it in the field, and the panel stays
open for the other fields; {k:Esc} leaves the panel and the field as they
were.  The entry Another file... opens a list of files and does not finish the
panel.  Every other list of choices, such as the list of printers of a print
panel, takes the entry with {k:Ins} or {k:Enter} and leaves the panel open.
.pic pictures/file-new-list.png 5 The list that New shows, with the description of the chosen template below it. [file-new-list-figure]
The list is shown in {fig:file-new-list-figure}: the template that is chosen is
the German window envelope, and its description is in the three lines below the
list.
.h3 The templates that come with Strata [file-new-shipped]
.ix Letter templates
.ix Envelope templates
.ix Templates that come with Strata
Strata comes with templates for business letters and for envelopes.  The
layout is Strata's own.  The templates that come with Strata hold the page
set-up, the size of the text, the named formats and the blocks as empty
paragraphs, and no text of their own: the new document has no address or
sentence that someone else wrote.  The one field in them is the date of a
letter.  A template made by the user can hold any text that every document of its
kind should start with, such as the return address of an envelope or the name
of a company: write that document, give it the description that the list
shows, and save it in a directory of {x:file-templates-path}
({n:file-templates-create}).  Each template has a description, so that the
list shows what it is.
.table 20 40
Template | What it is
{c:letter-us} | A business letter on US Letter paper.
{c:letter-a4} | The same letter on A4 paper; the recipient stands where the
 | window of a DL window envelope shows it (DIN 5008, form B).
{c:envelope-us-ca-10} | A number 10 envelope, 4 1/8 by 9 1/2 inches, for the
 | United States and Canada.
{c:envelope-us-ca-9} | A number 9 envelope, 3 7/8 by 8 7/8 inches, for the
 | United States and Canada.
{c:envelope-us-ca-monarch} | A Monarch envelope, number 7 3/4, 3 7/8 by 7 1/2
 | inches, for the United States and Canada.
{c:envelope-uk-dl} | A DL envelope, 110 by 220 millimetres, for the United
 | Kingdom.
{c:envelope-uk-c5} | A C5 envelope, 162 by 229 millimetres, for the United
 | Kingdom.
{c:envelope-uk-c6} | A C6 envelope, 114 by 162 millimetres, for the United
 | Kingdom.
{c:envelope-de-din5008-dl} | A DL window envelope for Germany: the address field
 | of the standard DIN 5008, form B, in the window of DIN 680.
{c:envelope-fr-dl} | A DL envelope for France, with the address block in the
 | right half, as La Poste asks.
{c:envelope-eu-dl} | A DL envelope for continental Europe and Russia.
{c:envelope-eu-c5} | A C5 envelope for continental Europe and Russia.
{c:envelope-eu-c65} | A C6/5 envelope, 114 by 229 millimetres, for
 | Switzerland and other European countries.
{c:envelope-latam-dl} | A DL envelope, the sobre americano, for Latin America.
{c:envelope-au-dl} | A DL envelope for Australia, in the address zone of
 | Australia Post.
{c:envelope-au-c5} | A C5 envelope for Australia, in the address zone of
 | Australia Post.
{c:envelope-jp-yougata-4} | A Western-style envelope, Yougata 4, 105 by 235
 | millimetres, for Japan, addressed horizontally.
{c:envelope-jp-yougata-2} | A Western-style envelope, Yougata 2, 114 by 162
 | millimetres, for Japan, addressed horizontally.
.end
A letter has the parts of a business letter in the style of the LaTeX letter
class: the address of the sender at the right, the date below it, the name
and address of the recipient at the left, the salutation, the paragraphs of
the letter with a blank line between them and no indent, the closing and the
signature at the right with room for a signature, and below them the lines
{c:cc:}, {c:encl:} and {c:P.S.} (carbon copy, enclosures and postscript).  Every part has a named format of its own
({c:Sender}, {c:Date}, {c:Recipient}, {c:Salutation}, {c:Body}, {c:Closing},
{c:Signature}, {c:Enclosure}, {c:Copy} and {c:Postscript}), so that a change
of look is one change of the format.  The first page has no head; the pages
after it have the head {c:date - page}; add the name of the recipient to it
in Global, Page.  The blocks are empty: type the text into them, in the
order of the parts, and delete the parts that the letter does not need, such as
the lines {c:cc:}, {c:encl:} and {c:P.S.} (carbon copy, enclosures and postscript).  The cursor starts in the block of
the sender.
An envelope is a page of its own size, turned to lie wide.  The sender, the
return address, stands 7 mm from the left edge and 7 mm from the top edge of
every envelope that has one, measured to the first line in the printed page:
a laser printer prints to about 4 mm from the edge.  The recipient stands
where the post of the country expects it.  Where a country has no template of its own,
take the template of the same size: the sizes of continental Europe, Latin
America and Australia are the sizes of the ISO series.  The place of the
recipient in the templates of the United Kingdom, Germany, France, Australia,
the United States and Japan follows the rules of the postal service or the
standard of the country; the templates of continental Europe and Latin America
keep the margins of Royal Mail for it, because no rule of their own was found.
In the German window envelope the window shows the recipient only: the sender
is in the corner.  A Japanese envelope has no
sender block, because Japan Post prompts for the sender on the back of the
envelope.  The rules of a postal service may differ; move the blocks to the
place that the service requires.
The table gives the place of the first line of each block, in millimetres
from the left edge and the top edge of the envelope.
.table 23 18 18
Template | Sender | Recipient
{c:envelope-us-ca-10} | 7, 7 | 102, 52
{c:envelope-us-ca-9} | 7, 7 | 95, 46
{c:envelope-us-ca-monarch} | 7, 7 | 89, 46
{c:envelope-uk-dl} | 7, 7 | 100, 45
{c:envelope-uk-c5} | 7, 7 | 110, 70
{c:envelope-uk-c6} | 7, 7 | 70, 48
{c:envelope-de-din5008-dl} | 7, 7 | 25, 68
{c:envelope-fr-dl} | 7, 7 | 113, 50
{c:envelope-eu-dl} | 7, 7 | 100, 45
{c:envelope-eu-c5} | 7, 7 | 110, 70
{c:envelope-eu-c65} | 7, 7 | 100, 45
{c:envelope-latam-dl} | 7, 7 | 100, 45
{c:envelope-au-dl} | 7, 7 | 100, 45
{c:envelope-au-c5} | 7, 7 | 110, 70
{c:envelope-jp-yougata-4} | none | 28, 15
{c:envelope-jp-yougata-2} | none | 10, 15
.end
The first block of an envelope, the sender, is put by a block indent and by the
top margin of the page, which is as large as the distance from the top; the
recipient is put by a block indent and by a Whitespace marker between the two
blocks.  The cursor of a new envelope starts in the sender block (in the
recipient block of a Japanese envelope).  Change the indents, the margin and
the height of the marker to move a block.  The ruler of a document has 80 cells across, so the indent of a
block is counted in eightieths of the page width.  A printer cuts the
envelope by its own rules: put the envelope in the way that the printer
requires, and print a test page on plain paper first.
.h3 The sample envelopes [file-sample-envelopes]
.ix Sample envelopes
.ix Envelope samples
.ix Examples of envelopes
Strata also comes with one finished sample for each envelope template: the
envelope of the template filled in with the address of a person or a company
that does not exist, written as the country of the envelope writes it.
A sample shows what an envelope of each style looks like when it is filled in,
and it is a way to try the envelope on a printer before a real address is
typed.  The samples are in the directory {c:share/strata/samples/envelopes} of
the directory where Strata is installed.  A sample is an ordinary document, not a
template, so the list of New does not show it.
.table 26 34
Sample | The address
{c:sample-envelope-us-ca-10} | A company in the United States, with an
 | attention line and a ZIP+4 code.
{c:sample-envelope-us-ca-9} | A company in Canada, with the postal code of
 | Canada.
{c:sample-envelope-us-ca-monarch} | A person in the United States.
{c:sample-envelope-uk-dl} | A person in the United Kingdom.
{c:sample-envelope-uk-c5} | A company in the United Kingdom.
{c:sample-envelope-uk-c6} | A doctor in the United Kingdom.
{c:sample-envelope-de-din5008-dl} | A person in Germany, with the return line
 | in the window.
{c:sample-envelope-fr-dl} | A person in France, in capitals.
{c:sample-envelope-eu-dl} | A person in the Netherlands.
{c:sample-envelope-eu-c5} | A person in Italy.
{c:sample-envelope-eu-c65} | A person in Switzerland.
{c:sample-envelope-latam-dl} | A person in Mexico.
{c:sample-envelope-au-dl} | A person in Australia.
{c:sample-envelope-au-c5} | A company in Australia, with a post office box.
{c:sample-envelope-jp-yougata-4} | A company in Japan, written across, the
 | postal code first.
{c:sample-envelope-jp-yougata-2} | A person in Japan, written across, the
 | postal code first.
.end
To open a sample, choose Retrieve in the File menu of the Editor, or Edit in
the Document Manager, and type the name of the file with its directory.  A
sample opens like any document, as in {fig:file-sample-edit-figure}: a
Whitespace marker puts the line of the sender where it belongs, a second one
puts the address, and the lines of the address follow.  Print it as you print
any document.  {fig:file-sample-print-figure} shows the sample of the number
10 envelope as the Printer prints it.  To make an envelope from a
sample, change the address and save the document with another name.
.pic pictures/edit-envelope-sample.png 5 A sample envelope in the Editor: the line of the sender, the Whitespace marker, and the address. [file-sample-edit-figure]
.pic pictures/print-envelope-sample.png 5 The sample of the number 10 envelope, as it is printed. [file-sample-print-figure]
The text of an envelope is larger than the text of a letter: the templates and
the samples are set in 14 points (the German window envelope in 11, because the
return line of its window is small by design, and the European C5 in 12).  The
Preview shows an envelope as it is printed, the long side across, as in
{fig:file-sample-preview-figure}.  An envelope chosen in Global, Page, Paper
Size is stored that way; a page set upright can be turned with an Orientation
marker, and the part of a block or of a section after such a marker is turned
too.
.pic pictures/view-envelope-preview.png 5 The Preview of the number 10 envelope: the Viewer shows the page with the long side across. [file-sample-preview-figure]
.h3 Label sheets [file-new-labels]
.ix Label templates
.ix Avery labels
.ix Sticker sheets
.ix Templates, labels
Strata comes with a template for each of the common sheets of Avery
stickers: address, shipping, return address, filing, name badge, round,
disc and other labels, on US Letter paper and on A4.  A template is named
{c:label-us-} or {c:label-a4-} and the product number of the sheet in lower
case: {c:label-us-5160} is the sheet 5160 and {c:label-a4-l7160} is the sheet
L7160.  A sheet that Avery sells under several numbers has a template for each
number, with the same layout.  The description of a template starts with the
word Avery and the number, and shows the kind of label, the number of labels on
the sheet and the paper, for example: Avery 5160 address labels, 30 per
sheet, US Letter.
.h4 Finding a sheet [file-new-labels-find]
.ix Templates, finding by number
In the list of templates, type the number that is printed on the box of
labels.  The list chooses the first entry that has a word that begins with
what you typed, in its name or in its description, and the bottom line of the
list shows what you typed.  {k:Backspace} takes back the last character.  A
character that no entry can follow is ignored.  Any other key, such as
{k:Up} or {k:Down}, starts the search again.  Typing {c:5160} chooses
{c:label-us-5160}; typing {c:8160} chooses the template of the sheet 8160,
which has the layout of the sheet 5160.  {k:Enter} or {k:Ins} takes the
entry, as for any template.
.h4 Using a label template [file-new-labels-use]
.ix Labels, typing the text
A label template is a document of blocks, one for each sticker of the sheet,
and no text.  The page has the size of the sheet, and every block is placed so
that its first line is 2 mm from the left edge of its sticker and 1.5 mm from
the top edge (1 mm and 0.5 mm on a small sticker).  Type the text of each
sticker into its block, and move to the next block with the cursor keys.  The
blocks are filled down the first column of stickers and then down the next
column, not across the rows.  A block holds as many lines as fit on the
sticker: the column Lines of the table below gives the number, and the
template shows them as empty lines.  Type into those lines; do not add lines.
A line that is too long for the width of the sticker continues on the next
line.  If a sticker has more lines than it holds, the extra lines push the
stickers below it down, and every later sticker of that column is off its
place: make the text shorter.  The Preview shows the sheet as it is printed, so check it
there before a sheet of labels goes into the printer.
.pic pictures/file-new-labels.png 5 The list of templates after the number 5160 was typed: the chosen template is the sheet 5160. [file-new-labels-figure]
Strata also comes with a sample: the sheet 5160 filled in with thirty
addresses of people who do not exist.  It is in the directory
{c:share/strata/samples/labels} of the directory where Strata is installed, as
{c:sample-label-us-5160}, and is an ordinary document: open it, change the addresses and save it
with another name.  The printed sheet is in {fig:print-label-sample-figure}.
.pic pictures/print-label-sample.png 5 The sample sheet 5160 as it is printed: thirty addresses, each on its own sticker. [print-label-sample-figure]
.h4 Round and disc stickers [file-new-labels-round]
The place of a round sticker, or of a disc, is the square that holds it, and
the text of its block starts in the corner of the square that fits inside the
circle.  A disc has a hole in the middle that the template does not leave
free: keep the text of a disc to the lines that lie on the ring.
.h4 The sheets [file-new-labels-sheets]
The sizes and the numbers across and down are those of the sheets that Avery
publishes in its templates.  The table gives, for each sheet, the product
numbers that have the layout, the kind, the paper, the number of stickers
across and down, the size of one sticker in millimetres, and the number of
lines that a sticker holds.
.include labels-table.src
A printer can place the page a little off.  Print the sheet on plain
paper first and hold it against a sheet of labels in front of a light.  If
every sticker is off by the same amount, change the margins in Global, Page
by that amount.  The template does not name a printer.  The sizes and the
numbers of the sheets can differ by a millimetre from the numbers of the
printed packet.
.h3 The structured sample [file-sample-story]
.ix Structured sample
.ix How Strata was made
.ix Example, structured document
Strata comes with one long sample that is not a letter or an envelope: the
document {c:strata-story.doc} in the directory {c:share/strata/samples/story}, with
its pictures in a directory next to it.  It describes how Strata was made and
serves as an example.  It is a structured document with numbered sections four
levels
deep, a contents list, cross references, captions, tables, footnotes, notes,
an index, a header with the title of the section and a footer with the page
number.  Open it as {fig:edit-story-sample-figure} shows and use the outline
commands to see how it is built, then print it.  It prints with line
numbers, as a further example: the Line Numbering panel of Global Format
({n:format-fields-linenum}) has Numbering set to Pagewise, so that the count
starts again at 1 on every page, as the lines of a legal document are
numbered, and Number Every on 5, so that a number stands beside every fifth
line.  The rows of a table take no number.  To print the sample without the
numbers, set Numbering to Off on that panel and print again.  It is a sample,
not a template, so the list of New does not show it.
.pic pictures/edit-story-sample.png 5 The structured sample in the Editor: the title page and the start of the first section. [edit-story-sample-figure]
.h3 A letter and an envelope, step by step [file-new-example]
.ix Letter, example
.ix Envelope, example
This example makes a letter and the envelope for it.
1. Press {k:F12}, then {k:F} and {k:N}.  In the list of templates choose
{c:letter-us} and press {k:Enter}.
2. In the panel, type the name {c:offer} in Document Name and press {k:Ins}.
3. Type the lines of the sender, the lines of the recipient, the
salutation and the paragraphs in the empty blocks.  The date is a Date marker,
so it is the day of the print.  Change the name in the head of the second
page, which is a header that you reach with Global, Page.
4. Press {k:Ctrl-S}, then print the letter with {k:F12}, Print, Final.
5. For the envelope, choose New again and take the template of your country,
for example {c:envelope-de-din5008-dl}.  Type the name {c:offer-env}, type
the address into the two empty blocks, and print it in the same way, with the
envelope in the printer as the printer requires.
.h3 Sample letters [file-new-samples]
.ix Sample letters
.ix Letters, samples
Strata comes with sample business letters that use the letter style of the
templates {c:letter-us} and {c:letter-a4}: the named formats {c:Sender},
{c:Date}, {c:Recipient}, {c:Salutation}, {c:Body}, {c:Closing},
{c:Signature}, {c:Enclosure}, {c:Copy} and {c:Postscript}, with the same
settings as in the templates.  A letter adds a named format of its own only
for a part that the style has no name for: {c:Subject} for the subject line,
{c:Table head} and {c:Table row} for the rows of a table.  Every letter is
there twice, on US Letter paper (the name ends in {c:-us}) and on A4 paper
(the name ends in {c:-a4}).  On A4 paper the recipient stands where the
window of a DL window envelope shows it.
.table 20 40
Letter | What it shows
{c:enquiry} | A question to a supplier about a price.
{c:quotation} | The answer, with a table of prices.
{c:complaint-reply} | A reply to a complaint, with a {c:cc:} line.
{c:complaint-to-utility} | A customer's complaint to a utility about a bill.
{c:order-confirmation} | The confirmation of an order, with a {c:P.S.}.
{c:enclosures} | Documents that go with a letter: {c:encl:} and {c:cc:} lines.
{c:two-pages} | A letter of two pages, with the head of the second page.
{c:sans-serif} | A letter set in a sans serif face.
.end
The names, the companies and the addresses are made up.  The letter
{c:complaint-to-utility} is from a customer to a company whose name has an
ampersand, {c:Pathetic Gas & Deceptive}; the name is written and printed as
it is.  It is a firm and polite complaint about a bill that rests on an
estimated reading, with the number of the account and of the meter, a demand
for a corrected bill within 14 days, three enclosures and a copy.  The table
of the quotation has the borders of the tables of this manual: a header row in
bold with a double line under it, and a single line under every other row.
The letter in a sans serif face has the font of its text named {c:Text
(sans)}: the Printer sets the text in a sans serif face when the name of the
font has the word {c:sans} in it ({n:format-set-typefaces}).  The second
page of {c:two-pages} has a head with the name of the recipient, the date and
the number of the page.
{fig:file-letter-screen-figure} shows the letter {c:complaint-to-utility} on
the screen and {fig:file-letter-page-figure} the same letter as it is printed.
.pic pictures/file-letter-screen.png 5 The sample letter to the utility on the screen. [file-letter-screen-figure]
.pic pictures/print-letter-page.png 5 The same letter, printed on US Letter paper. [file-letter-page-figure]
To open a letter, start the Editor on its name, or choose Edit Document in the
Document Manager and type the name with its directory.  The letters are in the
directory
{c:share/strata/samples/letters} of the place where Strata was installed, for
example {c:/usr/share/strata/samples/letters}, and in {c:samples/letters} of
the source tree.  Save the letter under another name before it is changed, and
replace the sender, the recipient and the text.  The letters are made
by the program {c:tests/mkletters.py} of the source tree, so that a change of
the style is made once.
.h3 The New File panel [file-new-panel]
.ix New File, fields
.ix Document Name, New
.ix Template, field of New
.ix Revision, New
The panel has six fields.
.table 15 45
Field | Meaning and value
Working Directory | The directory of the new document and the first place
 | in which the template is looked for.  Shared by all
 | the File panels.  {k:F2} lists the directories.
Document Name | The name of the new document.  It is required and it
 | starts empty.  A name with no dot gets the extension
 | {c:.doc}.  {k:F2} lists the files that end with
 | {c:.doc}, so that you can see the names that are in
 | use.
Template | Structured, Unstructured, or the name of a template.  It
 | starts as {c:DEFAULT.SET}, or as the template that
 | was chosen in the list ({n:file-new-list}).  {k:F2} or
 | {k:Keypad +} lists Structured, Unstructured, the templates with
 | their descriptions, and Another file..., which looks for a file that
 | ends with {c:.doc} or {c:.set} in any directory.  When there is no
 | template, the list shows where the templates are looked for.  A name
 | is looked for in the places of {x:file-templates-path}, with
 | {c:.doc} or {c:.set} added if it has no extension.
Description | The description of the new document.  Left empty, the
 | description of the template is kept.
Author | The author of the new document.  Left empty, the
 | author of the template is kept.
Revision | The revision number.  It starts as 1.000.  It is
 | checked as for Save ({n:file-save-dar}) and
 | always used: the revision of the template is not
 | carried over.
.end
.h3 What happens after the panel [file-new-after]
.ix New, order of events
.ix The file exists.  Replace it when it is saved?
.ix New document type
.ix Cannot find the template file
.ix Template (message title)
When the panel is accepted, the Editor works in this order.  Each step can end
the command, and then nothing is changed.
.table 4 57
Step | What happens
1 | An empty Document Name is rejected with "No file name specified",
 | and a Revision that is not a number with "Not a valid revision number".
 | The panel stays open.
2 | The Working Directory is remembered.  The name gets {c:.doc} if it has
 | no dot.
3 | If a file of that name exists, the question "The file exists.  Replace it
 | when it is saved?" is asked.  No ends the command.  The file is not
 | touched at this point: the question only makes you decide now.  Saving the
 | new document later prompts "Overwrite old version on disk?" again.
4 | If the open document has unsaved changes, the question "The file has
 | changes that were not saved.  Ignore them?" is asked.  No ends the
 | command.  Yes throws the changes away; New does not offer to save them.
5 | The template is looked for.  If it is found it is read; a file that cannot
 | be read shows its reading error in a box titled "Template" (for example
 | "The file is not a document of this software.") and ends the command.
6 | If no file is found and the name is {c:DEFAULT.SET} (in any case of
 | letters), the built-in settings are used, and a menu titled "New document
 | type" prompts for Structured or Unstructured.  {k:Esc} ends the command.
 | Any other name that is not found shows "Cannot find the template file",
 | in a box titled "Template", and ends the command.
7 | The new document replaces the old one.  It has the name and directory of
 | step 2, the description and author (when typed) and the revision of the
 | panel.  The message line shows "New document".
.end
.pic pictures/file-new-type.png 4 The question that is asked when no template is found.
The new document is counted as changed: the status line shows an asterisk
before its name at once, because the name, the description and the revision
have been put in it, and Quit puts the question whether to save.  The cursor is at
the start.  The document that was open is not saved, not backed up and not
kept anywhere.
.caution New replaces the open document.  If you answer Yes to "Ignore
  them?" the changes of the open document are lost.  Save first ({n:file-save}) if you want to keep them.
.h3 New at the edges [file-new-edges]
.ix New, template with text
.ix New, unstructured template
.table 31 29
Case | Result
The template has text | The new document starts with that text.
The template is a file of Save Global | The new document is empty (one empty
 Settings Only | block) with the settings of the
 | template.
The template is unstructured | The new document is unstructured; the
 | choice of "New document type" is not
 | offered, because it is part of the
 | template.
A template name with a slash | The path is used as given, relative to the
 | directory in which the Editor was started.
Template field empty | "Cannot find the template file".
The file {c:DEFAULT.SET} exists in the | It is used, and no question about the
 working directory or in the template | type of the document is asked.  Put the
 directory | file there to get the same settings for
 | every new document.
.end
.h3 See [file-new-see]
{X:file-templates} on templates and where they are found, {x:file-new-ways} on the other ways to begin a document, and the Document
Manager's own Edit / Preview / Print Document, which prompts for the type of the
document, the
description and the author.

.h2 Delete [file-delete]
.ix Delete
.ix Delete File panel
.ix Erasing a file
.ix Removing a file
.ix F12, File, Delete
.ix Delete, no way back
Delete erases a file from the disk.  It is the one command of the File menu
that touches a file that is not the document: the file is named, and, after
a question, it is gone.
.table 20 40
Way to reach it | Notes
{k:F12}, File, Delete | Press {k:F12}, then {k:F} and {k:D}.
.end
The menu hint reads "Delete a document file from the disk".  The command
works on any file that can be removed, not only on documents.
.pic pictures/file-delete.png 5 The Delete File panel.
.h3 The Delete File panel [file-delete-panel]
.ix Delete File, fields
.ix File Name, Delete
The panel has two fields.
.table 15 45
Field | Meaning and value
Working Directory | The directory in which the file is looked for; shared
 | by all the File panels.  {k:F2} lists the directories.
File Name | The name of the file, complete: no extension is added
 | ({n:file-names-ext}).  It starts empty and
 | must not be empty.  {k:F2} lists all the files of the
 | Working Directory, whatever their names end with.
.end
A name has no wildcards: the characters {c:*} and {c:?} are taken as part of
the name, so that one file is erased at a time.
.h3 What happens after the panel [file-delete-after]
.ix Delete, confirmation
.ix Delete, messages
.ix Cannot find the file
.ix Cannot delete the file
.ix Deleted (message)
.pic pictures/file-delete-confirm.png 4 The question that Delete puts.
.table 4 57
Step | What happens
1 | An empty File Name is rejected with "No file name specified".
2 | The Working Directory is remembered.  If there is no file of the name, a box
 | titled "Delete" says "Cannot find the file" and the command ends.
3 | The question "Delete {i:name}?" is asked, with the bare name of the file
 | (without its directory) as the question.  Yes is the first answer.  {k:N}
 | or {k:Esc} cancels.
4 | The file is removed.  If that fails, the box titled "Delete" shows "Cannot
 | delete the file"; if it is done the message line says "Deleted" and the
 | full name.
.end
There is no way to get a file back after it has been deleted.
.h3 Delete at the edges [file-delete-edges]
.ix Delete, the open document
.ix Delete, directory
.ix Delete, link
.table 31 29
Case | Result
The file is the document that is open | It is erased.  The document stays in
 | the Editor with its name and its
 | changes; the next Save, or {k:Ctrl-S},
 | writes it again.
A backup file ({c:.bak}, in {c:.backup}) or a template | Erased like any file.
A directory | "Cannot delete the file": directories are not
 | removed.
A link | The link is removed, not the file that it points to.
A file in a directory that cannot be | "Cannot delete the file".
 changed |
A name with no extension that is typed | "Cannot find the file" when only
 while the file is {c:report.doc} | {c:report.doc} exists.
.end
.h3 See [file-delete-see]
{X:file-checklist} on the checklist of all files, and {x:file-backup} on the backup file that Save keeps, which is the safeguard
that Delete does not have.

.h2 Import [file-import]
.ix Import
.ix Import ASCII Text File panel
.ix F12, File, Import
.ix Plain text, importing
.ix Text file, importing
.ix ASCII file, importing
.ix Character set, Import
.ix Line endings, Import
Import reads a plain text file and inserts its text into the document at the
cursor.  It reads text only: text from other word processors, spreadsheets or
databases has to be saved by them as plain text first.
.table 20 40
Way to reach it | Notes
{k:F12}, File, Import | Press {k:F12}, then {k:F} and {k:I}.
.end
The menu hint reads "Import an ASCII text file".
.pic pictures/file-import.png 5 The Import ASCII Text File panel.
.h3 The Import ASCII Text File panel [file-import-panel]
.ix Import ASCII Text File, fields
.ix ASCII File Name, Import
.ix Ignore Line Endings
.ix Preserve Line Endings
The panel has four fields.
.table 15 45
Field | Meaning and value
Working Directory | The directory in which the file is looked for; shared
 | by all the File panels.  {k:F2} lists the directories.
ASCII File Name | The name of the text file, complete: no extension is
 | added.  It starts empty and must not be empty.  {k:F2}
 | lists all the files of the Working Directory.
Options | How the line ends of the file are read: Ignore Line
 | Endings or Preserve Line Endings.  Default: Ignore
 | Line Endings.
Character Set | The character set of the file: IBM Extended ASCII
 | (CP 437), 96 Character ASCII (7 Bit), IBM Multilingual
 | (CP 850), Portuguese (CP 860), Norwegian/Danish
 | (CP 865), UTF-8 or Latin-1 (ISO 8859-1).
 | Default: IBM Extended ASCII (CP 437).
.end
.h3 The Options field [file-import-options]
.ix Import, line ends
.ix Import, paragraphs
.ix Import as a document
A line end in the file is a Line Feed, or a Carriage Return followed by a Line
Feed.  The two values treat it differently.
.table 24 36
Value | Effect
Ignore Line Endings | The file is read as prose.  A line end between two
 | lines of text is replaced by one space, so that the
 | lines of a paragraph run together and are wrapped
 | again by the Editor.  A blank line (an empty line, or
 | one that has only spaces) ends the block, and a new
 | block starts; any number of blank lines in a row
 | make one break.  Blank lines at the start of the file
 | are ignored.
Preserve Line Endings | Every line end of the file becomes a line break in
 | the text, so that the lines stay as they are.  Blank
 | lines are kept.  The whole text goes into the current
 | block, which is the way to import data in columns, a
 | listing or a table that is laid out with spaces.
.end
Ignore Line Endings is for letters, notes and articles; Preserve Line Endings
is for what is arranged by its lines.  In both, the spaces and the tabs of the
file are kept.  A Tab of the file becomes a tab of the text.
.pic pictures/file-export-charset.png 4 The list of the Character Set field (the same on the Import and Export panels).
.h3 The Character Set field [file-import-charset]
.ix Import, code pages
.ix Code page, Import
.ix CP 437, CP 850, CP 860, CP 865
.ix UTF-8, Import
.ix Latin-1, Import
.ix Characters not kept, Import
.ix Control characters, Import
The Editor holds its text in IBM Extended ASCII, code page 437.  Import
changes the characters of the file from the chosen code page to it.
.table 26 34
Value | Treatment of the characters of the file
IBM Extended ASCII (CP 437) | Taken as they are.
96 Character ASCII (7 Bit) | Only the 7 bit part of each character is kept.
IBM Multilingual (CP 850) | Each character is changed to the letter of CP 437
 | that has the same shape.
Portuguese (CP 860) | The same for CP 860.
Norwegian/Danish (CP 865) | The same for CP 865.
UTF-8 | The file is read as UTF-8 text.  Each character that the document
 | can show is put in the text; any other character becomes a
 | question mark.
Latin-1 (ISO 8859-1) | Each character of the file is the character of Latin-1
 | that has the same shape in the document; any other becomes
 | a question mark.
.end
A letter of CP 850, 860 or 865 that has no shape in CP 437 becomes a question
mark.  A control character is not put in the text as it is: it is written as
the two characters {c:^} and the letter, so that Control-A is {c:^A}; the Tab
is the exception.
With UTF-8 and Latin-1 Import counts the characters that it could not keep.
The accented letters of the western languages, the box characters and the
no-break space are kept, and so are the Greek letters of mathematics and the
other signs that the document can show ({n:appchar-high}).  A Chinese
character, a letter of an alphabet that the document does not have, or an
emoji becomes a question mark, one for each character.  When the import is
done the message line shows how many were not kept, for instance "Imported;
3 characters not kept"; when none was lost the message line shows "Imported"
and the name of the file, as for the other sets.  The older sets do not count.
A mark that UTF-8 files may have at the start, to say that the text is
UTF-8, is not text and is skipped.  A part of a UTF-8 file that is not valid
UTF-8, for instance a file that is Latin-1 or that was cut, is not
refused: each such part is read as the Latin-1 character with that number, and
it is a question mark that is counted when the document has no place for it.
.caution A question mark that Import made stays in the document, and the
  character that it stands for is gone from the text.  Keep the imported file
  until the text has been checked, when the message line has reported
  characters that were not kept.
.h3 Where the text goes [file-import-where]
.ix Import, cursor
.ix Import, document changed
The text is inserted at the cursor, as if it had been typed.  When it has been
inserted the cursor is put back at the start of the new text, so that the
beginning of the new text is in view.  The document is marked as changed.  The
message line shows "Imported" and the full name of the file, or, for UTF-8 and
Latin-1 when characters were not kept, how many.
The text that was after the cursor in the block follows the new text.  With
Ignore Line Endings, the end of the last line of the file is joined to it
without a space or a break, unless the file ends with a blank line, which
starts a new block there.  With Preserve Line Endings, the last line end of
the file, if the file has one, is a line break before that text.
An empty file inserts nothing.
.h3 Import at the edges and messages [file-import-edges]
.ix Import, messages
.ix Import failed
.ix Cannot open the file
.ix Read error
.table 31 29
Case | Result
The ASCII File Name is empty | "No file name specified"; the panel stays.
There is no such file | Box "Import failed": "cannot open file".
The file is read, but not completely | Box "Import failed": "read error".
The name is a directory | Box "Import failed": "this is a directory, not a
 | file".
The name is a device | Box "Import failed": "this is not a regular file".
The file cannot be examined | Box "Import failed": "cannot read file".
A file that is a Strata document | Its content is inserted as text.  Use
 | Retrieve for documents.
A long line | There is no limit of the length of a line;
 | the Editor wraps it.
.end
.h3 See [file-import-see]
Retrieve & Insert after Current Section ({n:file-retrieve}) to add a Strata
document to a document; Export ({n:file-export}) for the way back; the chapter
on conversion for the other ways to bring text in.

.h2 Export [file-export]
.ix Export
.ix Export ASCII Text File panel
.ix F12, File, Export
.ix Plain text, exporting
.ix Screen image
.ix Export Data Only
.ix Export a Screen Image
.ix Export a LaTeX File
Export writes the document as a plain text file, for a program that does not
read documents of Strata: a mail program, a text editor, a word processor
that takes text.  The text has CR LF line ends.  The attributes (bold,
underline), the fonts, the page layout and the pictures do not come into the
text file.  A third choice, Export a LaTeX File, writes the file that Print
and Preview give to LaTeX, for a person who wants to work on it with LaTeX;
{x:file-export-latex} describes it.
.table 20 40
Way to reach it | Notes
{k:F12}, File, Export | Press {k:F12}, then {k:F} and {k:E}.
.end
The menu hint reads "Export an ASCII text file".
.pic pictures/file-export.png 5 The Export ASCII Text File panel.
.h3 The Export ASCII Text File panel [file-export-panel]
.ix Export ASCII Text File, fields
.ix ASCII File Name, Export
.ix Scope, Export
.ix Export Entire Document
.ix Export Current Section
The panel has five fields; Scope is shown only when the document is
structured.  When Options is Export a LaTeX File a sixth field, Check
Validity, is shown.
.table 15 45
Field | Meaning and value
Working Directory | The directory in which the file is written; shared by
 | all the File panels.  {k:F2} lists the directories.
ASCII File Name | The name of the file to write, complete: no extension
 | is added (but see Export a LaTeX File).  It starts empty
 | and must not be empty.
 | {k:F2} lists the files of the Working Directory.
Options | What is written: Export a Screen Image, Export Data
 | Only or Export a LaTeX File.  Default: Export a Screen
 | Image.
Character Set | The character set of the file: the same seven values
 | as the Import panel.  Default: IBM Extended ASCII
 | (CP 437).  Export a LaTeX File does not use it.
Scope | Export Entire Document or Export Current Section.
 | Default: Export Entire Document.
Check Validity | Active only for Export a LaTeX File; with the other options
 | it stays in its place, dim, and the cursor skips it.  No or Yes:
 | Yes also runs LaTeX on the file and reports what it finds
 | ({n:file-export-latex-check}).  Default: No.
.end
.h3 The Options field [file-export-options]
.ix Export, screen image
.ix Export, data only
.ix Export, columns and tables
.ix Export, line width
.table 24 36
Value | What the file holds
Export a Screen Image | The text as the Editor lays it out on a screen of 80
 | columns, with the borders of the blocks and without
 | the rules between blocks: each block is wrapped into lines that are at
 | most 79 characters long, with a blank line between
 | blocks.  Headlines have their numbers.  The indent of
 | each level, centered and right aligned text, the
 | columns of a block side by side and the cells of a
 | table are as they are on the screen.  Markers show
 | as they do on the screen: a keycap as its text between
 | spaces, an equation, a picture, white space or a page
 | break as a name in square brackets, and the others
 | in their short form in square brackets, such as [NOTE], or as a
 | diamond when the display mode says so.  The bullet of a list is written as an
 | asterisk.  A border is drawn as the screen draws it,
 | with the box characters of the character set,
 | the corners, tees and crossings too (in Latin-1 and
 | in ASCII, which have none, a plus sign, a hyphen, an
 | equals sign for a double line, and a vertical bar):
 | the line above the first block and below the
 | last one, the lines between two blocks and the
 | sides on every row.
Export Data Only | Only the text, and none of the layout: each block is
 | one line that is as long as the text (a line end is
 | written only at the end of the block or at a line
 | break of the text), with real Tab characters.  Each
 | column of a block is written on a line of its own, so
 | that a table is taken apart cell by cell.  Headlines
 | have no numbers.  A marker that has a text or a value
 | (a keycap, for instance) is written as text; the
 | others (equations, pictures, page breaks, white space)
 | are left out.  The bullet of a list is an asterisk.
Export a LaTeX File | The LaTeX file that Print and Preview typeset, with
 | its pictures in a directory beside it ({n:file-export-latex}).
.end
Use a screen image when the columns and tables must stay in line and the
receiving program does not wrap the text; use data only when the receiving
program does the wrapping and the layout.
.note A screen image is made of what the screen shows.  A marker that the
  Editor draws as a diamond (its display mode is Diamond) and the sign of a text indent (the mark that a
  bullet has before its text) are written as control characters.  They show
  in the exported file as odd characters, or as {c:^D} and {c:^Z} in a
  program that shows control characters.  A program that cannot take them
  must be given a file of Export Data Only, which has none of them.
.h3 The Scope field [file-export-scope]
.ix Export, current section
.ix Export, title page
Export Entire Document writes all the sections.  Export Current Section writes
the section that the cursor is in, with all its subsections; the section
numbers of a screen image start at 1 for that section, as in an extract of
Save ({n:file-save-options}).  The title page, which is the text above
the first level, is never written: it is not part of the text for either
value.  A LaTeX file is different: Export Entire Document writes the title
page with the rest, as Print does.
.h3 The Character Set field [file-export-charset]
.ix Export, code pages
.ix Export, UTF-8
.ix Export, Latin-1
.ix Characters without a shape in a code page
The characters of the text are changed from CP 437 to the code page that is
chosen.  A character that the page does not have becomes a question mark.  For
96 Character ASCII (7 Bit), the accented letters become the letter without the
accent (an e with an accent is written as e; the Spanish n with a tilde as n).
A few others have a letter or a digit: the one-half and one-quarter signs
become 1, the superscript n becomes n, the superscript 2 becomes 2, and the
no-break space becomes a space.  Every other character above the 7 bit range
becomes a question mark.
UTF-8 loses nothing: every character of the document is written as the same
character in UTF-8, the accented letters, the Greek letters and the box
characters included, and the no-break space is written as a no-break space.
The bullet of a list is still an asterisk, as in every set.  Latin-1 has the
accented letters of the western languages and the no-break space; a Greek
letter, a box character and the other signs that Latin-1 lacks are written as
a question mark, one for each character.  The lines end with a Carriage Return
and a Line Feed in every set.
.h3 Export a LaTeX File [file-export-latex]
.ix Export a LaTeX File
.ix LaTeX file, exporting
.ix LaTeX file, compiling
.ix .tex file
.ix Pictures directory of an exported file
.ix latex_pictures
.ix latex_positions
.ix Exporting a document with errors
Export a LaTeX File saves the file that Print and Preview give to LaTeX, so
that it can be kept, changed or sent to somebody who works with LaTeX.
It is the same file that Print typesets: LaTeX makes of it the pages of a
Final print, except that an exported file has no banner, no marks of errors
and no Error Log.  When the document has conditional text, the file holds only
the text of the branches that the variables choose ({n:marker-if-print}).  The
Scope field works as for the text files, and a name that has no extension gets
{c:.tex}.  The Character Set field is not used.
.h3 Checking that LaTeX accepts the file [file-export-latex-check]
.ix Check Validity
.ix LaTeX file, checking
.ix Exporting, checking the file with LaTeX
The field Check Validity is active when Options is Export a LaTeX File; with
the other options it stays in its place, dim, and the cursor skips it.
It is No at first.  With No the file is only written: LaTeX is not run, the
export
is quick, and a file that LaTeX cannot process, for example because a picture
is damaged, is found only when the file is compiled; Strata does not report
it.  With Yes the export also runs LaTeX once on the file, as Print does,
and reports what it finds.  While LaTeX runs a box shows "Checking the file with
LaTeX", and {k:Esc} stops the check: the file stays, and the message line shows
that the check was stopped.
When LaTeX accepts the file the message line shows "Exported" and the full name
of the file, followed by "LaTeX accepts it".  When it does not, the message
line shows how many errors there are (together with those of the document, as
for any export), and a box lists the first of them with their places in the
document, such as a picture that LaTeX cannot read.  The file is written
whatever the check finds: it is neither removed nor changed.  If LaTeX is not
installed, the message line shows "not checked" and what to install, and the
file is exported all the same.  The check takes about as long as a print of
the document.
.h3 Making the pages of an exported file [file-export-latex-compile]
To make the pages, run LaTeX on the file twice, so that the page numbers of
the references settle:
.code
pdflatex report.tex
.end
The file works with {c:latex} too, followed by {c:dvipdfmx} when a PDF is
wanted.  The comment at the top of the file says what made it, from which
document and on which day, how to compile it, and which packages it needs;
they are all in a standard TeX Live installation.  The end of the file lists
its pictures.  The file is made again each time the document is exported:
a change made in the file is lost then, so make the change in the
document.
.h3 The pictures of an exported file [file-export-latex-pictures]
Every picture of the document is copied into a directory beside the file, named
as the file with {c:_files} added (the file {c:report.tex} has the directory
{c:report_files}).  The file names the pictures in that directory, so the file
and the directory can be moved or sent together; keep them together.  A picture
of PostScript is also made into a PDF there, because LaTeX reads that.  A
document without pictures makes no directory.  At each export the picture files
of the last export (the names that begin with {c:pic} and a number) are
removed from the directory first; other files in it are left alone.
.table 19 41
Setting | Effect
{c:latex_pictures}=keep | The file names each picture where it is, and copies
 | nothing (a PostScript picture is still copied).  The default, copy,
 | copies them.
{c:latex_positions}=yes | The file keeps, before each line, a comment that says
 | which line of the document it came from.  By default these comments are
 | left out.
.end
The two settings are keys of the file {c:config} ({n:appcfg-keys}); the Setup
panel has no field for them, so they are changed by editing the file.
.h3 Errors, and what an exported file is for [file-export-latex-errors]
A document with errors is exported all the same.  An equation or a picture
that LaTeX would reject is written as text, as Print writes it.  The message
line shows "Exported" and the name of the file, followed by "with 3 errors and
1 warning" when there are some, and a box lists the first of them with their
places.  The document gets no Print Error markers from an export, and Find
Next Error has nothing to find after one; the Error Log is not added to the
file.  If LaTeX is not installed on the system, the export still works, but
which equations LaTeX would reject is not known: they are found when the
file is compiled.  The field Check Validity ({n:file-export-latex-check})
runs LaTeX on the file and reports the errors that it finds.
Strata does not read an exported file back: it is made for LaTeX.  Keep the
document itself as the source, and export again when the document changes.
.h3 After the panel [file-export-after]
.ix Export, overwrite
.ix Export failed
.ix Exported (message)
.table 4 57
Step | What happens
1 | An empty ASCII File Name is rejected with "No file name specified".
2 | The Working Directory is remembered.  If a file of that name exists, the
 | question "Overwrite old version on disk?" is asked, with Yes and No.
3 | The file is written.  If it cannot be written, the box "Export failed"
 | shows "cannot write" and the full name of the file.  If the text is not
 | completely written, the box shows "write error".  Otherwise the message line
 | says "Exported" and the full name.
.end
For Export a LaTeX File the message line shows "Exported" and the full name,
and when the document has errors or warnings it adds how many; with Check
Validity set to Yes it ends with "LaTeX accepts it" or reports what LaTeX does
not accept.  If the directory of the pictures cannot be made the box "Export
failed" says so.
.h3 Export at the edges [file-export-edges]
- The document is not changed by Export, and its name, revision and changed
  mark stay as they were.
- The written file is not a document: Retrieve rejects it with "The file is
  not a document of this software."; use Import to bring it back.
.h3 See [file-export-see]
Import ({n:file-import}) for the way in; Save with Extract Current Section
({n:file-save-options}) for a part of a document that stays a document; the
chapter on printing for text that is made by the Printer.

.h2 Messages of the File commands [file-messages]
.ix Messages, File
.ix Error messages, File commands
.ix File messages
The File commands put words on the screen: the reasons that the panels refuse a
value, the boxes that report a failure, the questions, and the lines of the
message line.  Each is listed here with the command and what to do.  The chapter on messages lists all the messages of the programs.
.h3 Panel refusals [file-messages-panel]
.ix Panel refusals
A panel that rejects a value shows a box with the title of the panel and the
reason (see {n:file-panels-check}); press a key and the panel is still open.
.table 18 10 30
Message | Commands | What to do
No file name specified | All six | Type the name in the name field.
Not a valid revision number | Save, New | Type digits with at most three
 | | decimals, for instance 2 or 1.25.
.end
.h3 Boxes that report a failure [file-messages-fail]
.ix Failure boxes
.ix Message boxes, File
These boxes have a title, a line of text and the words "Press any key".  The
command is over when the key is pressed, and its panel is not shown again.
.table 10 18 30
Title | Text | Cause and remedy
Save failed | cannot write file | The directory or the file
 | | does not permit writing.
 | | Change the Working
 | | Directory or the name.
Save failed | cannot write {i:path} | The same for Save Global
 | | Settings Only and Extract.
Save failed | write error | The disk is full or failed.
Save failed | There is no current | Extract Current Section
 | section | and the cursor is in no
 | | section.
Retrieve failed | cannot open file | No such file, or it cannot
 | | be read.  Check the name,
 | | the Working Directory.
Retrieve failed | read error | The file could not be read
 | | through.  Try again.
Retrieve failed | The file is not a document of this software. | The file is
 | | not a document.  Use Import.
Retrieve failed | The file is version N.  This software reads up to version 3.
 | | The file was written by a newer version of the software.
Retrieve failed | The file version is N.  This software reads versions 2 to 3.
 | | The version of the file is below the oldest that is read, or it is
 | | damaged.
Retrieve failed | damaged document: a piece of text has no end | The file is
 | | damaged; use a backup.
Template | Cannot find the | New: the template is not
 | template file | there.  See
 | | {x:file-templates-path}.
Template | the reading error of | New: the template is not a
 | Retrieve | document.
Delete | Cannot find the file | The name is not a file of
 | | the Working Directory.  Add
 | | the extension.
Delete | Cannot delete the file | A directory, or a
 | | directory that is
 | | protected.
Import failed | cannot open file | No such file.
Import failed | cannot read file | The file could not be examined.
Import failed | read error | The file could not be read.
Import failed | this is a directory, not a file | The name is a directory.
Import failed | this is not a regular file | The name is a device.
Export failed | cannot write {i:path} | The directory does not
 | | exist or is protected.
Export failed | write error | The disk is full or failed.
.end
.h3 Questions [file-messages-questions]
.ix Questions, File
.table 34 26
Question | Asked by
Overwrite old version on disk? | Save, Export: when the file exists.
The file exists.  Replace it when it | New: when the name is that of a
 is saved? | file.
The file has changes that were not | Retrieve (not Insert) and New:
saved.  Ignore them? | the open document has changes.
Delete {i:name}? | Delete.
New document type | New, with no template: Structured
 | or Unstructured.
.end
.h3 The message line [file-messages-line]
.ix Message line, File
After a command has done its work the message line, above the status line,
shows one of these lines.  {i:path} is the full name of the file.
.table 26 34
Line | After
Saved {i:path} | Save, and {k:Ctrl-S}.
Retrieved {i:path} | Retrieve, every value.
New document | New.
Deleted {i:path} | Delete.
Imported {i:path} | Import.
Imported; {i:N} characters not kept | Import with UTF-8 or Latin-1: N
 | characters became question marks (1 character).
Exported {i:path} | Export.
.end

.h2 Examples [file-examples]
.ix File examples
.ix Examples, File
.ix Worked examples, File
The chapter has five short examples.  The names in them are examples.
.h3 Saving a document under a new name, keeping the old [file-examples-saveas]
.ix Save As, example
.ix Version of a document, keeping
The document {c:budget-proposal-draft-1.doc} is continued in a copy, so that
the first draft stays as it is.
.table 4 57
Step | What to do
1 | Press {k:F12}, then {k:F} and {k:S}.  The Save panel opens with the Document
 | Name {c:budget-proposal-draft-1.doc}.
2 | Type {k:Down}, and type {c:budget-proposal-draft-2.doc}.  The first key
 | replaces the old name.
3 | Press {k:Ins}.  There is no such file, so there is no question.  The
 | message line shows "Saved" and the full name.  The document is now called
 | {c:budget-proposal-draft-2}.
.end
The file of draft 1 is as it was when it was last saved, and further changes
and saves go to draft 2.  If the revision of the document was 1.004, it was
raised to 1.005 in the file of draft 2 and the file of draft 1 still has 1.004.
.h3 Making a template and using it [file-examples-template]
.ix Template, example
.ix Memo template, example
A memo has the page layout, the typefaces and the headlines wanted for all
memos.
.table 4 57
Step | What to do
1 | Choose File, Save.  Move to Document Name and type {c:memo}.  Move to
 | Options and choose Save Global Settings Only.  Press {k:Ins}.  The file
 | {c:memo.set} is written with the settings and no text.
2 | Put {c:memo.set} in the directory that the environment variable
 | {c:STRATA_TEMPLATES} names, or leave it in the working directory.
3 | Later, choose File, New.  Type {c:memo-to-staff} for the Document Name and
 | {c:memo.set} for the Template (or choose it with {k:F2}).  Type your name
 | in Author.  Press {k:Ins}.  A new document, with the settings of the memo,
 | opens.
.end
To make the file {c:DEFAULT.SET} that the New panel proposes, save the
settings under that name; New then needs no question about the type.
.h3 Getting an older version back [file-examples-backup]
.ix Backup, example
.ix Restoring, example
Backups Kept is 1 or more, and the document was found damaged after the last
save.  The newest backup of {c:annual-report.doc} is
{c:.backup/annual-report.bak01}.
.table 4 57
Step | What to do
1 | Leave the Editor.  In the Document Manager choose Restore Backup, and give
 | the Document Name {c:annual-report.doc}.
2 | Choose backup 01, then View the text, and read it.  If it is the right one,
 | press {k:Esc}, choose Restore this backup and answer {k:Y}.
3 | The damaged version is now backup 1, in {c:.backup/annual-report.bak01};
 | you can get it back the same way, because a restore is a swap.
.end
.h3 Adding a standard text to a document [file-examples-insert]
.ix Retrieve Insert, example
.ix Standard text, example
A file {c:terms-and-conditions.doc} holds the standard terms; a contract needs
them as the last section.
.table 4 57
Step | What to do
1 | Put the cursor in the last section of the contract.
2 | Press {k:Ctrl-R}, type {c:terms-and-conditions.doc} in the Document Name,
 | move to Options and choose Retrieve & Insert after Current Section.  Press
 | {k:Ins}.
3 | The sections of the terms are added after the last section.  The contract
 | is marked as changed; nothing was replaced.  Save it when the result is
 | right.
.end
.h3 Moving text to and from a text editor [file-examples-text]
.ix Import, example
.ix Export, example
The minutes arrive as a text file, {c:minutes.txt}, with a line end at every
line, in a plain code page.  They are put in a document, edited and sent back
as text.
.table 4 57
Step | What to do
1 | In a new document choose File, Import.  Type {c:minutes.txt} as the ASCII
 | File Name, keep Ignore Line Endings and the Character Set that matches the
 | file, and press {k:Ins}.  The paragraphs, which were broken at every line,
 | are wrapped as paragraphs again.
2 | Edit, and save the document as usual.
3 | To send it back as text choose File, Export.  Type {c:minutes-edited.txt}.
 | Choose Export Data Only if the other program wraps the lines itself, or
 | Export a Screen Image if the layout must stay.  Press {k:Ins}.
.end
If the file has accented letters and came from a modern system, it is
usually in UTF-8: choose UTF-8 as the Character Set of Import
({n:file-import-charset}), and of Export when the text goes back.
